  "summary": "I wrote the endless runner in `src/main.ts`. It typechecks, but I haven't run it in a browser or run `vite build`, so the visuals, controls and `window.__TASK_STATE__` values are untested.\n\n- **Gameplay:** The runner stays still while the track dashes, side posts, trees and hurdles scroll toward the camera. Speed rises from 8 m/s to 20 m/s over 500 m. Jumping works from Space, ArrowUp, W or a click/tap. It has a gravity arc and no double jump. Hurdles have random heights and gaps from a seeded generator (default seed 12345, or `?seed=N`), so a given seed repeats. Hitting a hurdle sets `lost`, and reaching 500 m sets `won`.\n- **Restart:** R, or a jump input after the run ends, resets the run to distance 0 with status `ready`. That also reseeds the generator, so every restart plays the same hurdle layout. I chose `ready` over starting play immediately because the task left it open.\n- **HUD:** A DOM bar under the canvas shows distance, session best and status. An overlay appears over the canvas when the run is `ready` (start hint) and on `won`/`lost` (restart hint).\n- **State and rendering:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated every frame. The `speed` field is 0 whenever the run isn't `playing`, including after `won` or `lost`. `window.__TASK_READY__` is set after the first frame. A `requestAnimationFrame` loop runs the game logic, and Aura3D's own loop does the drawing. The canvas isn't resized because I passed `resize: false` to `createAuraApp`.\n\n",
